The following points are raised from time to time by students. It is, perha=
ps, worthwhile to make an announcement.

(0) If one looks at graded or ungraded exams from PREVIOUS STUDENTS of mine=
 that I have NOT provided, one is doing so at one's OWN RISK. Firstly, what=
 happened in a previous term may have been overridden by some announcement =
this term and ONLY students, observers, and teachers, as listed under peopl=
e can see them. Secondly, I should VERY STRONGLY advise against this practi=
ce as it led to BLIND COPYING in the past. If SEVERAL students make the SAM=
E ERROR on any problem, particularly a c problem, it is immediately obvious=
 what is going on and PENALTIES will be SEVERE.

(1) On any page, if there are blanks to be filled in or items to be circled=
, then these actions must be performed. If the space provided for work is l=
arge enough to accommodate all the work that needs to be shown in the handw=
riting of the student in question, then one may do it there. If one squishe=
s everything into the area using microscopic handwriting that I cannot read=
 (and at times I have used a magnifying glass with no success), then I will=
 award a credit of at least 0 and possibly smaller numbers, depending on th=
e nature of the submission. In general, it is desirable to show work on pag=
es that follow. But one should always note the answer on the same page if t=
here is a designated place for the answer.

(2) I will NOT discuss exam problems with current students. I will not look=
 at work that current students have done on their exam problems.

(3) One may NOT include BLANK PAGES on which one has not attempted anything=
 or on which one has shown no work. (-1 per blank page )

(4) The single document that one submits should have the pages in order, pr=
operly numbered. The first sheet should be the top sheet. The resolution MU=
ST be reduced so that it is of the appropriate size (<25MB). ONE MUST CHECK=
 WHAT ONE HAS SUBMITTED TO MAKE SURE THAT IT MEETS THE REQUIREMENTS.

(5) The set of natural numbers denoted by =E2=84=95 is defined to be the se=
t: {0, 1, 2, 3, ... } and contains 0.

(6) The set of whole numbers, denoted by =E2=84=A4 is defined to be the set=
: { ..., -3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, ... }.

(7) In a certain subset of the English speaking world the convention is to =
omit 0 from the "natural numbers", as if there is something 'unnatural' abo=
